Security, secrets, and hardening.

What is covered. This notebook is the deep dive on container security. The container threat model — what container isolation protects against, and what it explicitly does not. The kernel primitives revisited — namespaces, control groups, capabilities, sec-comp, and app-armor or ess-ee-linux. The root-in-container problem — why running as root is almost never necessary, and what it lets an attacker do. Running as non-root — the user instruction in a dockerfile, the user flag at run time, and fixing file ownership. User-namespace remapping — daemon-level user-I-D translation, with user-n-s remap. Rootless docker — running the daemon itself unprivileged. Linux capabilities — drop all, then add what you need. The standard hardened baseline. Sec-comp profiles — restricting which system calls a container can make. App-armor and ess-ee-linux — mandatory-access-control layers, and the default docker-default profile. Read-only root filesystem — the read-only flag with t-m-p-f-s overlays. No-new-privileges — blocking set-user-I-D escalation. Secrets — why environment variables are not secret, and what to use instead. Compose, swarm, and build-kit secrets — the three places real secrets live in the docker ecosystem. Image signing — cosign, docker trust, in-toto attestations. And supply-chain hygiene — software bills of materials, provenance attestations, and the path from docker build to a signed, scanned, provenanced artifact.

By the end you should be able to write a hardened dockerfile, run a container with the surface area an auditor will approve of, and explain to a teammate why dash e secret equals something is a bug, not a feature.

The container threat model.

Before stacking defences, be clear about what containers are and are not.

Containers are a process-isolation mechanism, not a security boundary. They keep workloads from accidentally tripping over each other on a shared kernel. They are not designed to safely host untrusted, hostile code on a multi-tenant kernel. That is what virtual machines, and projects like kata containers and g-visor, are for.

What container isolation protects against by default. A container reading or modifying another container's files — each has its own mount namespace. A container seeing another's processes — each has its own process-I-D namespace. A container hogging all the see-pee-you and ram — control groups limit per-container resources. And a container's process binding the host's privileged ports — network namespace, plus dropping the net-bind-service capability if you want.

What it does not protect against, by default. A container running as root that breaks out via a kernel vulnerability, of which there are several every year. A container with the privileged flag doing essentially anything to the host. A container that gets sys-admin capability mounting host filesystems or escaping via control groups. A container that mounts the docker socket — that is remote control of the daemon, which means root on the host. And a malicious or compromised image — supply-chain attacks.

Hardening equals adding layers of constraint until the surface area is small enough that a compromise in the container is unlikely to compromise the host.

The kernel primitives revisited.

From notebook one, docker composes linux kernel features. Quick reminder of what each one buys you.

Namespaces isolate views of the system — process-I-D, mount, network, inter-process communication, you-t-s, user, and control group. What makes a container a container. Control groups account and limit resources — see-pee-you, memory, input output, process I-Ds. Notebook three covers the memory and see-pee-yous flags. Capabilities slice the all-powerful root into around forty individual privileges — net-bind-service, sys-admin, and so on. Sec-comp lets you allow-list or block-list specific system calls. The default profile blocks around fifty dangerous system calls. App-armor and ess-ee-linux are mandatory-access-control on top of file permissions. Distro-dependent. Docker ships a docker-default app-armor profile. And user namespaces map user-I-Ds inside the container to different user-I-Ds on the host — root in the container can be user-I-D one-hundred-thousand on the host.

The default configuration docker gives a container is already much tighter than running the same process directly on the host. Forty-plus capabilities dropped — only around fourteen retained by default. Around fifty system calls blocked by the default sec-comp profile. And app-armor's docker-default profile applied on systems with app-armor.

But "much tighter than nothing" is not the same as "tight enough for production." The rest of this notebook is "how much further you should crank each dial."

The root-in-container problem.

By default, the container's main process runs as root inside its namespace — user-I-D zero. People often think "well, it is just root inside the container, that does not matter." It mostly does.

Root in the container can modify any file in the container's filesystem, including ones bind-mounted from the host as root. Bind to privileged ports inside the container. Use any capability the container still has — which is a lot in the default configuration. Use a kernel c-v-e — and there are several every year — to escape into host context. And read its own credentials, including secrets passed in environment variables.

Plus, files the container creates on a bind-mounted host directory are owned by user-I-D zero on the host, unless you have configured user-namespace remapping. So a "root in container" with a host bind mount is effectively root on those files on the host.

The fix is mundane and effective — do not run as root. Most containerised apps do not need to.

Running as non-root.

Two places to set the user.

In the dockerfile. Start with from python tag three point twelve slim. Do the installation steps as root. Then run user-add dash-dash system dash-dash user-I-D ten-thousand-one dash-dash user-group dash-dash no-create-home app-user. Then user app-user. Then command, list of python and app dot p-y.

Best practice. Create a dedicated, system user for the app. Do not reuse nobody, user-I-D six-five-five-three-four — distros differ on what nobody is supposed to do. Pick a high user-I-D, like ten-thousand-one, that does not collide with common host user-I-Ds, like one-thousand, thirty-three, or nine-ninety-nine. The user instruction goes after all the steps that need root — package installation, file ownership. Switching too early forces awkward sudo patterns inside run.

At run time. Docker run dash u user-I-D, with an optional group-I-D, overrides the image's user. For example, docker run dash u ten-thousand-one colon ten-thousand-one my-org slash a-p-i. Or docker run dash u of i-d dash u colon i-d dash g, dash v p-w-d colon slash work alpine s-h, to run as your host user. This is the standard fix for the bind-mount file-ownership problem from notebook four — run the container as your host user-I-D and the files it creates have your host ownership.

But the image requires root. Some images insist on running as root — the official n-ginx, postgres for some operations, anything using port eighty or four-forty-three without net-bind-service capability. Two responses. Map the port. Run n-ginx as non-root inside the container listening on eighty-eighty. Publish with dash p eighty colon eighty-eighty. The privilege is at the host side, not the container. Use a non-root variant. N-ginx-inc slash n-ginx-unprivileged, bit-nami slash postgres-q-l. Many official images have non-root cousins. Or grant only the specific capability needed — cap-add of net-bind-service lets a non-root process bind to low ports.

User-namespace remapping.

By default, root inside a container is the same user-I-D zero as root on the host. They share the user namespace.

User-namespace remapping changes that. The daemon maps container user-I-D zero to, say, host user-I-D one-hundred-thousand. From the host's perspective, the container's root is just a normal unprivileged user. A breakout via a kernel bug still lands you as unprivileged.

Enable in the daemon configuration by setting user-n-s remap to default. That default creates a user called dock-remap on the host with a sub-user-I-D and sub-group-I-D allocation, and remaps all containers into that range.

Trade-offs. All containers share the same remap range. If you need per-container isolation, you need rootless docker, or kata. Bind mounts get tricky — files on the host owned by one-hundred-thousand colon one-hundred-thousand look like root inside the container. You have to change owner accordingly. And some features are incompatible — privileged, certain security options, sharing inter-process-communication or process-I-D namespaces with the host.

For dev or continuous integration, leave it off. For production servers running containers from sources you do not fully trust, turn it on.

Rootless docker.

User-namespace remapping still requires the daemon to run as root. Rootless docker runs the entire daemon, and all containers, under your unprivileged user account.

One-time setup is docker-d-rootless-setup-tool dot s-h install. Then system-control dash-dash user start docker. After setup, your shell has docker-host environment variable pointed at the rootless daemon. Docker run does what you would expect, but everything happens as your user. There is no root daemon to compromise. A kernel escape from a container only gives an attacker your own user-I-D's privileges on the host.

Trade-offs. Networking is slower by default — uses slip-four-net-n-s, a userspace proxy. Per-container network-host is unavailable. The privileged flag does not work, and should not. Control-group version two is required for resource limits. Most modern distros are fine. And no eye-p-tables rules on the host — rootless cannot write them. Port publishing works via slip-four-net-n-s instead.

Rootless is the right answer for shared dev hosts, sandbox environments, and continuous-integration runners where you do not want a continuous-integration step compromise to mean host compromise.

Linux capabilities.

The default container drops about half of linux's forty-ish capabilities. The remaining set is still pretty large and includes some that have led to escapes in the past — net-raw, set-user-I-D, dac-override.

The hardened baseline — docker run dash-dash cap-drop equals all dash-dash cap-add equals net-bind-service my-org slash a-p-i. Drop all capabilities, then add back exactly the ones the app needs. For most application code — nothing. For things that bind to low ports — net-bind-service. For things that need to change file ownership — chown.

In compose, you set cap-drop to a list of all, and cap-add to a list of net-bind-service.

Capabilities you should know. Net-bind-service is used for binding ports under one thousand twenty-four. Common for n-ginx and apache when running as non-root. Chown is used for change-owner outside your own user-I-D. Used by init scripts that fix file ownership. Set-user-I-D and set-group-I-D are used for changing user-I-D or group-I-D after start. Used by long-lived servers that drop privileges after binding. Kill, for sending signals across users — rare. Net-admin for configuring network interfaces — v-p-n containers, network appliances. Sys-admin is the "almost-root" capability — mount, swap, more. Avoid it. Almost never needed for app workloads. And sys-ptrace for strace and gdb — for debugging containers, not production.

The grand fail-safe — the privileged flag gives all capabilities and drops all the sec-comp and app-armor profiles. Use only when you can articulate why no other combination works — typically, running docker-in-docker, low-level hardware access, intrusion-detection-system containers.

Sec-comp profiles.

Sec-comp, secure computing mode, is a kernel feature that filters which system calls a process can make. Docker applies its default dot j-son sec-comp profile to every container, blocking about fifty system calls that have a history of c-v-e-s, or are not needed by normal apps — mount, un-mount-two, key-control, reboot.

For most containers the default is fine. To tighten further, docker run dash-dash security-opt sec-comp equals dot slash my-profile dot j-son my-org slash a-p-i. You can write a custom profile, j-son, allow-list style, that lets through only the system calls your app actually uses. Tools like o-c-i-sec-comp-b-p-f-hook, and falco, can trace your app under normal load and emit a minimal profile.

To disable sec-comp entirely — do not — docker run dash-dash security-opt sec-comp equals unconfined my-org slash a-p-i. This is what many "it does not work, I gave up" posts on stack overflow recommend. Resist. If a system call is blocked, the better fix is usually to fix the app, or add it to the profile.

App-armor and ess-ee-linux.

Two mandatory-access-control systems linux distros ship. They sit on top of regular file permissions and decide which paths, capabilities, and operations a process can perform.

App-armor — ubuntu, debian. Docker ships a docker-default profile applied automatically when app-armor is enabled. It restricts containers from writing to most of slash proc, mounting filesystems, raw networking, and so on. You can override with docker run dash-dash security-opt app-armor equals my-custom-profile my-org slash a-p-i.

Ess-ee-linux — red hat, fedora, cent-o-s. Different model, same idea. Type enforcement labels each file and process. Only matching labels can interact. Docker sets container labels with security-opt label equals something.

You usually do not touch either. They are applied by default. You tune them when an audit asks why a container has more access than it needs. If you do not have a specific reason to override, the defaults are correct.

Read-only root filesystem.

Most applications do not need to write to most of their filesystem at runtime. They write to specific paths — logs, caches, uploads. Marking the rest read-only blocks a huge class of post-compromise behaviours — dropping a web-shell, modifying binaries.

Docker run dash-dash read-only, dash-dash t-m-p-f-s slash t-m-p, dash-dash t-m-p-f-s slash var slash run, dash v app-logs colon slash app slash logs, my-org slash a-p-i. The read-only flag makes the container's root filesystem read-only. The t-m-p-f-s slash t-m-p gives slash t-m-p writable in ram — most apps assume slash t-m-p is writable. The t-m-p-f-s slash var slash run is for things like process-I-D files. And the volume on slash app slash logs is the persistent volume for things you actually want kept.

In compose, set read-only to true. Set t-m-p-f-s to a list of slash t-m-p and slash var slash run. And set volumes to the app-logs colon slash app slash logs entry.

This is one of the highest-leverage hardening moves available. A few minutes of audit work for a much smaller attack surface.

No-new-privileges.

A subtle linux feature. If a binary has the set-user-I-D bit, executing it normally elevates the process. No-new-privileges tells the kernel "ignore set-user-I-D bits for this process tree. It can never gain privileges beyond what it started with."

Docker run dash-dash security-opt no-new-privileges my-org slash a-p-i. In compose, set security-opt to a list including no-new-privileges colon true.

This breaks sudo, mount, and other set-user-I-D utilities inside the container. Most app containers do not need them, and breaking them is a positive. An attacker who lands in your container cannot suddenly become root via a set-user-I-D binary.

Combine with non-root user and capability dropping for the hardened baseline. The compose service has user ten-thousand-one colon ten-thousand-one, read-only true, t-m-p-f-s list of slash t-m-p and slash var slash run, cap-drop list of all, cap-add list of net-bind-service, and security-opt list of no-new-privileges true.

Secrets — and why environment variables are not secret.

A pattern from every tutorial — docker run dash e d-b-password equals hunter-two my-org slash a-p-i. Convenient. Not secret. That password leaks in at least four places. First, docker inspect prints the environment block. Anyone with daemon access reads it. Second, slash proc slash p-i-d slash environ on the host exposes it to anyone who can read it — root, often more. Third, the shell history on the launcher's machine. Fourth, crash dumps and log statements that helpfully include "environment at the time of crash."

Plus, environment variables are inherited by child processes. Every shell-out inside the container sees the password too.

The proper container-native way is secret files mounted at a known path, with the file readable only by the app's user. Three places to get them from.

Docker compose secrets. In compose, the a-p-i service has a secrets list referencing d-b-password. At the bottom, the secrets block defines d-b-password with a file pointing at dot slash secrets slash d-b-password dot t-x-t. The file is mounted at slash run slash secrets slash d-b-password inside the container, with restrictive permissions. The app reads from the file path, not an environment variable.

Swarm secrets, in notebook nine. Same syntax in a compose-flavoured stack file. But swarm encrypts secrets at rest in the raft log, and distributes them only to nodes running services that need them.

Build-kit secret mounts, build-time. For secrets needed during the build — a private package registry token, an ess-ess-h key — start the dockerfile with the syntax pragma for dockerfile colon one point seven. Then in a run instruction, mount of type secret with i-d g-h-token. The script reads the token from slash run slash secrets slash g-h-token. Pair with docker build dash-dash secret i-d equals g-h-token source equals dollar home slash dot g-h dash token, dash t my-app. The token is mounted at slash run slash secrets slash g-h-token only for the duration of that run step. It is not committed to a layer. Compare with build-arg or environment for secrets — both bake the value into image history, where anyone who pulls the image can extract it.

External secret managers. For production, often you do not want the secret in any file on the deploy host. You want the container to fetch it at startup from vault, ay-double-you-ess secrets manager, or g-c-p secret manager. A sidecar or init container handles the fetch and writes to a t-m-p-f-s for the app to read. That is outside docker's scope but worth knowing exists.

Image signing.

How does the runtime know the my-org slash a-p-i tag one point two point three it just pulled was actually built by your continuous integration, and not swapped at the registry. Image signing.

Cosign — modern, widely-adopted. Part of the sig-store project. Sign with a private key, or with keyless github open-i-d-connect. Cosign sign dash-dash key cosign dot key, g-h-c-r dot eye-oh slash my-org slash a-p-i tag one point two point three. Verify with cosign verify dash-dash key cosign dot pub.

Signatures are stored as separate artifacts in the registry, associated with the image's digest. Kubernetes admission controllers — ky-vern-o, conn-aiss-eur — can refuse to schedule unsigned images.

Docker content trust — older. Built into the docker command-line client via the legacy notary version one system. Export docker content trust equals one. Then docker push my-org slash a-p-i tag one point two point three signs automatically. Docker pull my-org slash a-p-i tag one point two point three verifies. Fails if unsigned.

Less commonly used today in favour of cosign. Notary version two is in active development but not yet ubiquitous.

What signing buys you. Origin guarantees — you know the image was built by whoever holds the signing key. Integrity — re-pushes cannot impersonate. Signatures do not move automatically. And audit trail — you can prove which images were promoted through continuous integration by checking signatures.

Supply-chain hygiene — software bills of materials, provenance, attestations.

The newest layer in container security. Not just "is this image signed?" but "what is in it, and how was it built?"

Software bill of materials, or s-bom. A machine-readable list of every package, version, and license in an image. Two common formats — s-p-d-x, and cyclone-d-x. Generate with docker s-bom, syft, or build-kit's built-in support. Docker build-x build dash-dash s-bom equals true, dash t my-org slash a-p-i tag one point two point three, dash-dash push, dot. Inspect with docker build-x image-tools inspect, with a format directive that prints the s-bom as j-son.

The s-bom is attached as a separate artifact, referenced from the manifest. Downstream consumers can scan the s-bom directly without running the image.

Provenance attestations. A provenance attestation records how the image was built — the dockerfile, the build context's git commit, the builder image, the build arguments. Generated by build-kit with provenance equals true. Together with the s-bom, the consumer can answer "this binary came from this dockerfile, this source commit, this base image" in a verifiable way.

S-l-s-a — the framework that ties this together. S-l-s-a, supply-chain levels for software artifacts, is a google-originated framework that defines build maturity levels. Level one — provenance exists. Level two — provenance is signed. Level three — builds run on hardened, isolated builders. Level four — two-party review and hermetic builds.

Github actions, gitlab continuous integration, and most modern continuous integration systems can issue s-l-s-a level three attestations automatically. The chain is — git commit, to continuous integration build with provenance, to a signed image plus signed s-bom in the registry, to a cluster admission controller that verifies signature, s-bom is clean, and provenance is okay.

This is what modern production container security looks like today. Notebook ten, the install, troubleshoot, and dee-see-ay-prep chapter, revisits some of these as exam topics. This notebook is enough to know what each piece does and why it matters.

Putting it together — the hardened service.

A single compose service combining everything we have covered. The a-p-i service uses image g-h-c-r dot eye-oh slash my-org slash a-p-i at s-h-a two-five-six colon a-b-c, pinned by digest. User is ten-thousand-one colon ten-thousand-one, non-root. Read-only true, root filesystem read-only. T-m-p-f-s list of slash t-m-p and slash var slash run. Cap-drop list of all, no capabilities. Cap-add list of net-bind-service, just to bind low ports. Security-opt list of no-new-privileges true, no set-user-I-D escalation. Secrets list including d-b-password — secret file, not environment variable. Environment d-b-password-file equals slash run slash secrets slash d-b-password — the app reads from the file. Network is backend. Restart is unless-stopped. And a healthcheck that w-gets slash health every thirty seconds.

At the bottom, the secrets block defines d-b-password with file dot slash secrets slash d-b-password dot t-x-t. The networks block defines backend with driver bridge.

And on the registry side, the image referenced here is built from a multi-stage dockerfile, notebook two, producing a slim, no-toolchain runtime. Pinned by digest, so the deploy is byte-identical. Signed with cosign — admission controllers can verify origin. Accompanied by a software bill of materials — vulnerability scanners can audit packages. And accompanied by an s-l-s-a provenance attestation — auditors can verify the build chain.

That is the full picture. None of it is mandatory. All of it is achievable in a continuous integration pipeline of a few hundred y-a-m-l lines. The dee-see-ay exam, notebook ten, tests a subset of these — non-root, capabilities, secrets, signing — and they are the same things real auditors ask about.
